Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

register(RoundTrip);
define
    rtA;  rtClean;  rtB;  rtHost;  rtShare;  rtTwin;
    rtRa=9;  rtRc=9;  rtRb1=9;  rtRb2=9;
    rtRun code={
        fixRoundTripHere = 0;
        cerr "== ARM 0   PROBE CONTROL -- two adjacent probes, NOTHING between ==":;
        probeNode(rtA);
        probeNode(rtA);
        cerr "   the two node= above MUST match, or every address below is void":;

        cerr "== ARM A   ROUND TRIP ON ONE NODE -- the whole question ==":;
        cerr "   WRITE SITE:":;
        probeNode(rtA);
        rtA :. noPrinT;
        cerr "   READ SITE:":;
        probeNode(rtA);
        rtRa = rtA.noPrinT;
        cerr "ARM A   r =" rtRa "(MUST be 1)":;

        cerr "== ARM C   NEGATIVE CONTROL -- never written ==":;
        probeNode(rtClean);
        rtRc = rtClean.noPrinT;
        cerr "ARM C   r =" rtRc "(MUST be 0, else ARM A asserts nothing)":;

        cerr "== ARM B1  copyOf TWIN -- write the TWIN, read the ORIGINAL ==":;
        cerr "   original:":;
        probeNode(rtB);
        rtTwin <- copyOf(rtB);
        cerr "   twin:":;
        probeNode(rtTwin);
        rtTwin :. noPrinT;
        rtRb1 = rtB.noPrinT;
        cerr "ARM B1  original reads" rtRb1 "(copyOf COPIES the body)":;

        cerr "== ARM B2  addGroup TWIN -- write the TWIN, read the ORIGINAL ==":;
        cerr "   original:":;
        probeNode(rtShare);
        rtHost +% rtShare;
        cerr "   twin:":;
        probeNode(rtHost["rtShare"]);
        rtHost["rtShare"] :. noPrinT;
        rtRb2 = rtShare.noPrinT;
        cerr "ARM B2  original reads" rtRb2 "(addGroup SHARES the body)":;
        };
    ;
rtRun();
cerr "ROUNDTRIP SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

incant/roundTripT -- THE FLAG ROUND-TRIP ORACLE.
Built 2026-08-31 for Clay's SEQ round-trip brief, section C1.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

Three whole-split attempts, three reverts, and on the third the instrument
finally named something UPSTREAM of everything the first three searched.
incant/broadcastT's ARM 3 is its anti-vacuity control -- did the write land on
the original at all -- and under the split it FAILS. So the columns, the stamp,
the roads and the writers were all being measured through a void control.

    x :. noPrinT      then      x.noPrinT      does not round-trip.

If that is true, it is true with no copy road, no inheritance and no broadcast
anywhere near it, and it would produce the audit's stubborn 1 and the kant8T
hang no matter how perfect the copy law was. THE UNIT OF CHANGE HAD BEEN LARGER
THAN THE UNIT OF MEASUREMENT. This file is the smallest unit that can carry the
question.

HOW TO READ IT

    ARM A    write and read the SAME NAME. r MUST be 1. This is the question.
    ARM C    a node never written. r MUST be 0.
    ARM B1   write a copyOf twin, read the original.
    ARM B2   write an addGroup twin, read the original.

ARM C IS NOT DECORATION. Without it, ARM A reading 1 is consistent with a reader
that answers 1 for everything, which is the shape rule H4 exists to forbid. The
pair is the assertion; either row alone is not.

⚠ ANTI-VACUITY, AND IT IS THE BRIEF'S OWN CLAUSE: a run that prints no addresses,
or prints only the r values, is VOID and this fixture has failed to build. Say
so; do not score it. The addresses are what turn "the flag did not survive" into
"the flag was written to one node and read from another", and only the second
of those routes a repair.

⚠⚠ TWO CORRECTIONS TO THE BRIEF'S TREE CLAIM, BOTH CHECKED AT THE SITE BEFORE
ANY SCORING, PER ITS OWN ATTRIBUTION RULE

FIRST -- NEITHER ROAD GOES THROUGH A COPY, AND BOTH ARE AUTO-REPOINTED. The
brief's mechanism was "the write path goes through a copy; the shared body was
covering for it". Read at the site:

    write   Instruct.rtn opSetFlag  case 29    target.noPrint = true;
    read    Instruct.rtn opDot      case 29    if target.noPrint  product.count = 1;

Both act on `target` -- the operand the operator was handed -- and neither mints,
copies or unwraps anything on the way. Both are BARE tok-resolved spellings, so
a field moving from GroupBody to GroupItem re-points BOTH on the next retok, by
the same mechanism docs/groupBodySplit.md section 1 measures. AT THE SOURCE
LEVEL THE SETTER AND THE READER CANNOT COME APART.

So if ARM A fails under the split, the divergence is NOT between the two
switch cases. It is in what `target` IS at each site -- that is, in the road
that resolves a NAME to a NODE -- or in the copy constructor, or in how
`options` is initialised. That is a different repair from the one the brief's
row 2 routes to, which is why the check comes before the scoring.

SECOND -- `copyOf` IS NOT THE SHARING ROAD, SO ARM B1 ALONE WOULD ASSERT
NOTHING. The brief's mechanism sentence says "the copy constructor shares
groupBody", and that is right; but the arm it specifies uses `copyOf`, which
COPIES the body. docs/groupBodySplit.md's own second-attempt census is explicit
about the two roads and which does which. A write through a copyOf twin should
fail to reach the original under BOTH layouts, so that arm cannot discriminate
bare from split -- it is a control, not a probe. ARM B2 is therefore added, on
the addGroup road, which is the one that actually shares a body and the one
incant/broadcastT's ARM 2b already fires in the opposite direction.

Both arms are kept. B1 is the brief's row, honestly run and honestly labelled as
the control it turns out to be; B2 is the probe it was reaching for.

MUTATION IS BY DIRECT SUBSCRIPT, READING IS THROUGH A CAPTURE

ARM B2 writes with `rtHost["rtShare"] :. noPrinT;` rather than through a `<-`
capture, because copy-on-rebind is a RULED DEFECT: a `<-` hands back a copy, so
a write through one can confirm itself and change nothing. Bear-trap 35 states
the rule and this file obeys it.

⚠⚠ ARM 0 IS THE ARM THAT MATTERED, AND IT VOIDED THE COLUMN IT WAS GUARDING

Two adjacent probeNode calls on the same name, with NOTHING between them, report
DIFFERENT node= addresses and the SAME body=. So probeNode's own road mints a
fresh wrapper per call, and the node= column in ARMs A, B1 and B2 CANNOT be read
as evidence about what `:.` and `.` see. It is not wrong; it is about probeNode.

That is the brief's own attribution rule biting the instrument rather than the
claim, and it is the third outcome a two-outcome prediction has nowhere to put:
not pass, not fail -- VOID. Reported as void rather than graded.

WHAT SURVIVES ARM 0, and it is not nothing: the body= column is STABLE across
every call and every arm on a given field, and DIFFERENT between fields. So the
naming road does not preserve node identity, and the BODY is what is stable.

THE ADDRESSES THAT DO ANSWER THE QUESTION COME FROM THE TWO SITES THEMSELVES

Ephemeral source instrument, run 2026-08-31, then reverted and the binary
rebuilt BARE before any capture was taken. Re-applying it is thirty seconds:

    Instruct.rtn  opSetFlag case 29, above the assignment
        -% ::fprintf(stderr,"RTWRITE noPrint target=%p body=%p\n",
                     (void*)target,(void*)target->groupBody); %-
    Instruct.rtn  opDot     case 29, above the test
        -% ::fprintf(stderr,"RTREAD  noPrint target=%p body=%p\n",
                     (void*)target,(void*)target->groupBody); %-

Both go inside braces on the case, so the tok-resolved line beside them still
re-points under a split. Neither passthrough reads a flag field, so the
instrument is layout-agnostic and can be carried across the move unchanged.

⚠ It is NOT left in the tree. It prints on every `:.` and every `.noPrinT`, so
it would move fleet baselines the moment anybody ran a POP against it. The
recipe is the artifact; the code is not.

VERDICT, FIRST RUN 2026-08-31, ON THE BARE BINARY

    ARM 0   node= DIFFERS between two adjacent probes, body= same   COLUMN VOID
    ARM A   RTWRITE target=0x104f74c00  body=0x104f68280
            RTREAD  target=0x104f77e80  body=0x104f68280
            r = 1
    ARM C   r reads false                                            control holds
    ARM B1  write body 0x104f86000, read body 0x104f6cf50  DIFFERENT bodies
            original reads false                                     control holds
    ARM B2  write body 0x104f6ccd0, read body 0x104f6ccd0  SAME body
            original reads 1

⚠⚠ THE WRITE AND THE READ LAND ON DIFFERENT NODES SHARING ONE BODY. Under the
legacy layout, on the bare binary, with no split anywhere near it. The round
trip works TODAY for exactly one reason: the flag lives in the shared GroupBody.

⚠⚠ AND ARM A AND ARM B2 ARE THE SAME PHENOMENON. Naming a field twice is, at
the node level, indistinguishable from taking an addGroup twin -- two nodes, one
body, in both. The thing the campaign has been calling "broadcast" and the thing
it has been calling "a round trip" are one mechanism seen from two sides.

THAT IS ROW 3 OF THE BRIEF'S TRUTH TABLE: addresses differ, r reads 1 in R1 --
the covering mechanism confirmed DIRECTLY. Row 3 takes no R2 column, by the
table's own construction, so R2 is a don't-care for the scoring and was NOT run.

⚠ AND IT SETTLES noPrint's OWN GATE WITHOUT MOVING IT. Section C4 requires
`R2 r == 1` before a flag may move. A bit resident on the NODE and written to
node X cannot be read back from node Y; the naming road is independent of where
flags live, so no layout change alters it. noPrint therefore CANNOT meet its
gate, and C4's own clause applies: a flag that fails the gate STAYS in the body
and gets a register row naming the row it hit. That row is written in
docs/groupBodySplit.md.

⚠ ARM C READING `noPrinT` IS THE CORRECT FALSE READING, NOT A DEFECT. opDot mints
`product = new(tag)` and only sets `product.count` when the flag is on, so a
false read hands back a field with no data -- and a field with no data returns
its own tag (bear-trap 26). Distinct from 1 and distinct from the uninitialised
9, which is all a control has to be. Do not "fix" it into a 0.

NEXT: OPEN

This is an oracle, not a citizen -- it has no fix to recommend because it exists
to find out which repair is owed. What it produces is a row in the brief's C3
truth table, and each row routes somewhere different: to the layout in
GroupItem.twk, to the write path, to the copy constructor, or to a census before
any second flag moves.

WHAT WOULD GENERATE MORE: run it on the bare binary, then move `noPrint` alone
to GroupItem's options block and run it again. The two runs are the measurement.
Anything the table has no row for is filed OPEN and not guessed.
